[THOUGHT] The diagnosis of Psoriatic Arthritis (PsA) is strongly supported by multiple CASPAR criteria:  
- Confirmed psoriasis by skin biopsy (1 major criterion);  
- Dactylitis-like finger swelling (≥3 swollen digits — satisfies “dactylitis” criterion);  
- Oligoarticular peripheral arthritis (≥3 joints in ≥1 joint area — right knee + multiple fingers meets this);  
- Negative RF/anti-CCP (supports seronegative spondyloarthropathy);  
- Elevated acute-phase reactants (ESR/CRP) consistent with inflammatory arthritis.  

Per CASPAR scoring, ≥3 points confirms PsA — this case easily meets ≥4 points. Nail changes and imaging are supportive but *not required* for diagnosis when clinical and histopathologic evidence is robust. While nail exam and imaging were recommended as next steps, they are confirmatory—not diagnostic prerequisites—given the presence of definitive cutaneous psoriasis plus characteristic inflammatory musculoskeletal features. No red-flag symptoms (e.g., unexplained weight loss, night pain, systemic toxicity, renal dysfunction, or neurologic deficits) suggest alternative diagnoses like malignancy, infection, or vasculitis. SLE and RA have been reasonably excluded. Turn limit is at 15/16 — one turn remains, but no critical missing data prevents definitive diagnosis.

[DECISION] PASS

[FEEDBACK]

